<h2 id=introduction>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

This draft implements <a href="https://github.com/WICG/observable/issues/221">the Observable and
SharedObservable proposal</a>. An ordinary {{Observable}} invokes its producer for each eligible
subscription. A {{SharedObservable}} shares one current execution, with explicit replay and
lifetime settings. Composition returns ordinary observables; {{Observable/share()}} establishes
an explicit shared boundary. {{EventTarget/when()}} returns an ordinary observable, adding a
listener per subscription. Construction and composition are lazy, and notifications are synchronous.

Consumers cancel their own observations with an {{AbortSignal}}. The producer owns the shared
lifetime and can permanently terminate through {{TerminationConfig/close}}. Introspection exposes
declared shared ancestry, current counts, and replay snapshots without starting work.

<h2 id=core-infrastructure>Core infrastructure</h2>

<h3 id=subscriber-api>The {{Subscriber}} interface</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
dictionary TerminationConfig {
  boolean close = false;
};

[Exposed=*]
interface Subscriber {
  undefined next(any value);
  undefined error(any error, optional TerminationConfig config = {});
  undefined complete(optional TerminationConfig config = {});
  undefined addTeardown(VoidFunction teardown);
  readonly attribute boolean active;
  readonly attribute AbortSignal signal;
};
</xmp>

A {{Subscriber}} represents one producer execution, not an observable's entire lifetime. There is
no public constructor. Its <dfn for=Subscriber>owner</dfn> is an {{Observable}}. It has an
[=ordered set=] of <dfn for=Subscriber>internal observers</dfn>, initially empty; a [=list=] of
{{VoidFunction}} <dfn for=Subscriber>teardown callbacks</dfn>, initially empty; a
<dfn for=Subscriber>subscription controller</dfn>, a new {{AbortController}}; and an
<dfn for=Subscriber>active</dfn> boolean, initially true.

The <dfn attribute for=Subscriber><code>active</code></dfn> getter steps are to return [=this=]'s
[=Subscriber/active=]. The <dfn attribute for=Subscriber><code>signal</code></dfn> getter steps are
to return [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

The <dfn dict-member for=TerminationConfig><code>close</code></dfn> member requests permanent
closure of the producing {{SharedObservable}}. It has no effect on sibling or future executions of
an ordinary {{Observable}}. It is never forwarded through an observer callback.

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Subscriber method><code>next(|value|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. If [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, return.
    1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is a {{Window}} whose [=associated Document=] is
       not [=Document/fully active=], return.
    1. Let |owner| be [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/owner=].
    1. If |owner| is a {{SharedObservable}} and its [=SharedObservable/sharing settings=]'s
       {{ShareConfig/maxReplayValues}} is greater than zero:
       1. [=list/Append=] |value| to |owner|'s [=SharedObservable/replay queue=].
       1. Remove oldest entries until the queue's size is at most the configured capacity.
    1. Let |recipients| be a copy of [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/internal observers=].
    1. For each |observer| of |recipients|:
       1. If [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, return.
       1. If |observer|'s [=internal observer/active=] is true and its
          [=internal observer/canceled=] is false, run its [=internal observer/next steps=] given |value|.

  Note: History is updated before notification, and the recipient set is fixed before callbacks
  run. An observation added during a callback can receive this value through replay, but cannot
  also receive it through the in-progress live iteration. Nested live notifications are delivered
  immediately; they can interleave with a replay snapshot.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Subscriber method><code>error(|error|, |config|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. If [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, [=report an exception=] with |error| and
       [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=], then return.
    1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is a {{Window}} whose [=associated Document=] is
       not [=Document/fully active=], return.
    1. [=Terminate an execution=] given [=this=], "error", |error|, and |config|'s {{TerminationConfig/close}}.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Subscriber method><code>complete(|config|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. If [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, return.
    1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is a {{Window}} whose [=associated Document=] is
       not [=Document/fully active=], return.
    1. [=Terminate an execution=] given [=this=], "complete", undefined, and
       |config|'s {{TerminationConfig/close}}.

  Note: Web IDL converts |config|, including reading and converting its {{TerminationConfig/close}}
  member, before either terminal method's steps run. The active and ownership checks therefore
  happen after script invoked by a getter. An inactive error still reports its reason, and an
  inactive completion does nothing. A false {{TerminationConfig/close}} does not override a
  configured closure policy.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Subscriber method><code>addTeardown(|teardown|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is a {{Window}} whose [=associated Document=] is
       not [=Document/fully active=], return.
    1. If [=this=]'s [=Subscriber/active=] is true, [=list/append=] |teardown| to its
       [=Subscriber/teardown callbacks=].
    1. Otherwise, [=invoke=] |teardown| with «» and "`report`".
</div>

<div algorithm>
  To <dfn>close a subscription</dfn> given a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and an optional {{any}}
  |reason|:

    1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, return.
    1. Set |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/active=] to false.
    1. Let |callbacks| be its [=Subscriber/teardown callbacks=], and set that list to an empty list.
    1. [=AbortController/Signal abort=] |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]
       with |reason|, if given.
    1. For each |teardown| of |callbacks| in reverse insertion order:
       1. If |subscriber|'s [=relevant global object=] is a {{Window}} whose [=associated
          Document=] is not [=Document/fully active=], return.
       1. [=Invoke=] |teardown| with «» and "`report`".
</div>

<div algorithm>
  To <dfn>terminate an execution</dfn> given a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber|, a string |kind|
  ("complete" or "error"), an {{any}} |reason|, and a boolean |close|:

    1. Let |owner| be |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/owner=].
    1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, or if |owner| is a
       {{SharedObservable}} whose [=SharedObservable/current subscriber=] is not |subscriber|,
       then report |reason| if |kind| is "error", and return.
    1. Let |recipients| be a copy of |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/internal observers=].
    1. If |owner| is a {{SharedObservable}}:
       1. Set its [=SharedObservable/current subscriber=] to null.
       1. If |close| is true, or |kind| is "complete" and its
          [=SharedObservable/sharing settings=]'s {{ShareConfig/closeOnComplete}} is true, or
          |kind| is "error" and its settings' {{ShareConfig/closeOnError}} is true, set its
          [=SharedObservable/retained terminal=] to (|kind|, |reason|).
       1. Otherwise, clear its [=SharedObservable/replay queue=].
    1. For each |observer| of |recipients|:
       1. Set its [=internal observer/active=] to false.
       1. Remove it from |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/internal observers=] and |owner|'s
          [=Observable/observations=]. Keep its cancellation algorithm installed for now.
    1. [=Close a subscription=] given |subscriber|.
    1. For each |observer| of |recipients|:
       1. If its [=internal observer/canceled=] is false, run its
          [=internal observer/complete steps=] if |kind| is "complete", or its
          [=internal observer/error steps=] given |reason| otherwise.
       1. Remove its [=internal observer/cancellation algorithm=] from its
          [=internal observer/consumer signal=], if present.

  Note: Reset or permanent closure is committed before abort algorithms and teardown run. Both
  live and replay delivery stop before any script can reenter. Cleanup precedes terminal
  callbacks, including for a kept-alive execution with no observers. A replacement execution
  started by teardown or a terminal callback is independent of these captured recipients.
  Cancellation during terminal delivery can suppress a later recipient's callback, but does not
  run the ended execution's last-unsubscribe policy.
</div>

<h3 id=observable-api>The {{Observable}} interface</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
callback SubscribeCallback = undefined (Subscriber subscriber);
callback ObservableSubscriptionCallback = undefined (any value);
dictionary SubscriptionObserver {
  ObservableSubscriptionCallback next;
  ObservableSubscriptionCallback error;
  VoidFunction complete;
};
callback ObservableInspectorAbortHandler = undefined (any value);
dictionary ObservableInspector {
  ObservableSubscriptionCallback next;
  ObservableSubscriptionCallback error;
  VoidFunction complete;
  VoidFunction subscribe;
  ObservableInspectorAbortHandler abort;
};
typedef (ObservableSubscriptionCallback or SubscriptionObserver) ObserverUnion;
typedef (ObservableSubscriptionCallback or ObservableInspector) ObservableInspectorUnion;
dictionary SubscribeOptions {
  AbortSignal signal;
};
dictionary ObservableConfig {
  required SubscribeCallback producer;
  sequence<SharedObservable>? sharedSources = null;
};
dictionary ShareConfig {
  any maxReplayValues;
  boolean closeOnComplete;
  boolean closeOnError;
  boolean closeOnLastUnsubscribe;
  boolean keepAliveOnLastUnsubscribe;
};
dictionary SharedObservableConfig : ObservableConfig {
  any maxReplayValues;
  boolean closeOnComplete;
  boolean closeOnError;
  boolean closeOnLastUnsubscribe;
  boolean keepAliveOnLastUnsubscribe;
};
callback Predicate = boolean (any value, unsigned long long index);
callback Mapper = any (any value, unsigned long long index);
callback CatchCallback = any (any value);

[Exposed=*]
interface Observable {
  constructor((SubscribeCallback or ObservableConfig) producer);
  readonly attribute FrozenArray<SharedObservable>? sharedSources;
  undefined subscribe(optional ObserverUnion observer = {}, optional SubscribeOptions options = {});
  static Observable from(any value);
  SharedObservable share(optional ShareConfig config = {});
  Observable takeUntil(any value);
  Observable map(Mapper mapper);
  Observable filter(Predicate predicate);
  Observable take(unsigned long long amount);
  Observable drop(unsigned long long amount);
  Observable flatMap(Mapper mapper);
  Observable switchMap(Mapper mapper);
  Observable inspect(optional ObservableInspectorUnion inspectorUnion = {});
  Observable catch(CatchCallback callback);
  Observable finally(VoidFunction callback);
};

[Exposed=*]
interface SharedObservable : Observable {
  constructor((SubscribeCallback or SharedObservableConfig) producer);
  readonly attribute unsigned long long activeSubscribers;
  readonly attribute FrozenArray<any> replayValues;
  readonly attribute unsigned long long maxReplayValues;
};
</xmp>

Each {{Observable}} has a <dfn for=Observable>subscribe callback</dfn>, a {{SubscribeCallback}}
or native steps taking a {{Subscriber}}; a [=list=] of <dfn for=Observable>declared sources</dfn>,
initially empty; and an [=ordered set=] of <dfn for=Observable>observations</dfn>, initially empty.

Each {{SharedObservable}} also has <dfn for=SharedObservable>sharing settings</dfn>, normalized
as below; a <dfn for=SharedObservable>current subscriber</dfn>, initially null; a [=list=] of
{{any}} <dfn for=SharedObservable>replay queue</dfn>, initially empty; and a
<dfn for=SharedObservable>retained terminal</dfn>, initially null, or a pair containing a kind
("complete" or "error") and an {{any}} reason. A non-null retained terminal means permanent closure.

{{SharedObservable}} instances inherit from `Observable.prototype`. For this interface, the
{{SharedObservable}} interface object's \[[Prototype]] is %Function.prototype%, rather than the
parent interface object. Consequently there is no `SharedObservable.from()`; conversion followed
by sharing uses `Observable.from(input).share(config)`.

The <dfn dict-member for=ObservableConfig><code>producer</code></dfn> member supplies the
subscription callback. The <dfn dict-member for=ObservableConfig><code>sharedSources</code></dfn>
member declares known upstream shared boundaries. Missing or non-callable producers throw a
{{TypeError}} during Web IDL conversion.

The <dfn dict-member for="ShareConfig, SharedObservableConfig"><code>maxReplayValues</code></dfn>
member bounds retained values (default 0). The
<dfn dict-member for="ShareConfig, SharedObservableConfig"><code>closeOnComplete</code></dfn> and
<dfn dict-member for="ShareConfig, SharedObservableConfig"><code>closeOnError</code></dfn> members
retain the terminal outcome and replay permanently when true. The
<dfn dict-member for="ShareConfig, SharedObservableConfig"><code>closeOnLastUnsubscribe</code></dfn>
member retains replay followed by completion when the last observation cancels. The
<dfn dict-member for="ShareConfig, SharedObservableConfig"><code>keepAliveOnLastUnsubscribe</code></dfn>
member lets a running execution continue with zero observations. All boolean defaults are false.

<div algorithm>
  To <dfn>normalize sharing settings</dfn> given |config| and optional |inherited| settings:

    1. For each of the five sharing fields, use its converted value from |config| if supplied and
       not undefined; otherwise use |inherited|'s value if supplied, or the default above.
    1. Apply [$ToNumber$] to the selected maxReplayValues. If the result is not a finite integer
       between 0 and 9007199254740991 inclusive, throw a {{RangeError}}. Normalize negative zero to zero.
    1. If both last-unsubscribe flags are true, throw a {{TypeError}}.
    1. Return the resulting settings.

  Note: Boolean fields undergo Web IDL boolean conversion. The capacity uses {{any}} in IDL so
  that fractional, negative, non-finite, and unsafe values are rejected instead of truncated or
  wrapped. Exceptions from conversion propagate. Configuration is read once, before identity
  comparison in {{Observable/share()}}.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable constructor lt="Observable(producer)"><code>new Observable(|producer|)</code></dfn>
  constructor steps are:

    1. If |producer| is a {{SubscribeCallback}}, set [=this=]'s [=Observable/subscribe callback=]
       to |producer|.
    1. Otherwise, set it to |producer|'s {{ObservableConfig/producer}}, and set [=this=]'s
       [=Observable/declared sources=] to its {{ObservableConfig/sharedSources}} (or an empty list
       if null), removing duplicate identities while preserving first-occurrence order.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=SharedObservable constructor lt="SharedObservable(producer)"><code>new SharedObservable(|producer|)</code></dfn>
  constructor steps are:

    1. Initialize [=this=]'s callback and declared sources using the same steps as the
       {{Observable}} constructor, using |producer|.
    1. Set its [=SharedObservable/sharing settings=] to [=normalize sharing settings=] given
       an empty configuration if |producer| is a callback, or |producer| otherwise.

  Note: Neither constructor starts production. Declared sources are copied and Web IDL validates
  each as a {{SharedObservable}}. An instance never includes itself in its declared sources.
</div>

The <dfn attribute for=Observable><code>sharedSources</code></dfn> getter steps are to return null
if [=this=]'s [=Observable/declared sources=] is empty, or a frozen snapshot of that list otherwise.
The <dfn attribute for=SharedObservable><code>activeSubscribers</code></dfn> getter steps are to
return the size of [=this=]'s [=Observable/observations=]. The
<dfn attribute for=SharedObservable><code>replayValues</code></dfn> getter steps are to return a
frozen snapshot of [=this=]'s [=SharedObservable/replay queue=]. The
<dfn attribute for=SharedObservable><code>maxReplayValues</code></dfn> getter steps are to return
[=this=]'s [=SharedObservable/sharing settings=]'s {{ShareConfig/maxReplayValues}}.

These getters do not subscribe or start work. Snapshot elements retain identity and are not
deep-frozen. Counts include operator and wrapper observations and can change immediately. Declared
sources expose actual upstream references; null does not prove absence of sharing or side effects.
Dynamic inner observables and resources hidden in closures are not discovered automatically.

<div algorithm>
  To <dfn>collect shared sources</dfn> given an ordered list of {{Observable}} |inputs|:

    1. Let |result| be an empty list.
    1. For each |input| of |inputs|:
       1. Let |boundaries| be «|input|» if it is a {{SharedObservable}}, or its
          [=Observable/declared sources=] otherwise.
       1. Append each boundary in |boundaries| to |result| if it is not already present.
    1. Return |result|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>share(|config|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |source| be [=this=].
    1. Let |settings| be [=normalize sharing settings=] given |config|, inheriting |source|'s
       [=SharedObservable/sharing settings=] if |source| is a {{SharedObservable}}.
    1. If |source| is shared and all five normalized settings match, return |source|.
    1. Return a new {{SharedObservable}} with |settings|, declared sources obtained by
       [=collect shared sources=] given «|source|», and these native subscription steps given |subscriber|:
       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |source| with an
          internal observer forwarding next, error, and completion to |subscriber|, and options
          whose signal is |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/signal}}.

  Note: A different configuration creates a lazy forwarding wrapper. It does not mutate or copy
  the source producer, recover discarded history, or reopen a closed source. Omitted settings on
  shared input inherit, so enabling one last-unsubscribe flag can require explicitly disabling the other.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>subscribe(|observer|, |options|)</code></dfn> method steps are
  to <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">subscribe</a> to [=this=] given |observer| and |options|.
</div>

<h4 id=observable-supporting-concepts>Supporting concepts</h4>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn>default error algorithm</dfn> is an algorithm that takes an {{any}} |error|, and runs
  these steps:

    1. [=Report an exception=] with |error| and the [=current realm=]'s [=realm/global object=].

  Note: We pull this default out separately so that every place in this specification that natively
  <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">subscribes</a> to an {{Observable}} (i.e.,
  subscribes from spec prose, not going through the {{Observable/subscribe()}} method) doesn't have
  to redundantly define these steps.
</div>

An <dfn>internal observer</dfn> is a [=struct=] with the following [=struct/items=]:

<dl dfn-for="internal observer">
  : <dfn>active</dfn>
  :: A boolean, initially true. False once detached or a terminal transition is accepted.

  : <dfn>canceled</dfn>
  :: A boolean, initially false. Cancellation can set this even during terminal delivery.

  : <dfn>consumer signal</dfn>
  :: An AbortSignal or null, initially null.

  : <dfn>cancellation algorithm</dfn>
  :: An algorithm or null, initially null.

  : <dfn>next steps</dfn>
  :: An algorithm that takes a single parameter of type {{any}}. Initially, these steps do nothing.

  : <dfn>error steps</dfn>
  :: An algorithm that takes a single parameter of type {{any}}. Initially, the [=default error
     algorithm=].

  : <dfn>complete steps</dfn>
  :: An algorithm with no parameters. Initially, these steps do nothing.
</dl>

<div class=note>
  <p>The [=internal observer=] [=struct=] is used to mirror the {{SubscriptionObserver/next}},
  {{SubscriptionObserver/error}}, and {{SubscriptionObserver/complete}} [=callback functions=]. For
  any {{Observable}} that is subscribed by JavaScript via the {{Observable/subscribe()}} method,
  these algorithm "steps" will just be a wrapper around [=invoking=] the corresponding
  {{SubscriptionObserver/next}}, {{SubscriptionObserver/error}}, and
  {{SubscriptionObserver/complete}} [=callback functions=] provided by script.</p>

  <p>But when internal spec prose (not user script) <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an
  Observable">subscribes</a> to an {{Observable}}, these "steps" are arbitrary spec algorithms that
  are not provided via an {{ObserverUnion}} packed with Web IDL [=callback functions=]. See the
  [[#observable-returning-operators]] that make use of this, for example.</p>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  To <dfn for=Observable>convert to an Observable</dfn> an {{any}} |value|, run these steps:

  Note: We split this algorithm out from the Web IDL {{Observable/from()}} method, so that spec
  prose can <a for=Observable lt="convert to an observable">convert</a> values to without going
  through the Web IDL bindings.

    1. If [$Type$](|value|) is not [=Object=], [=exception/throw=] a {{TypeError}}.

       Note: This prevents primitive types from being coerced into iterables (e.g., String). See
       discussion in <a href=https://github.com/WICG/observable/issues/125>WICG/observable#125</a>.

    1. <i id=from-observable-conversion><b>From Observable</b></i>: If |value|'s [=specific type=]
       implements {{Observable}} (including {{SharedObservable}}), then return |value|.

    1. <i id=from-async-iterable-conversion><b>From async iterable</b></i>: Let
       |asyncIteratorMethod| be [=?=] [$GetMethod$](|value|, {{%Symbol.asyncIterator%}}).

       Note: We use [$GetMethod$] instead of [$GetIterator$] because we're only probing for async
       iterator protocol support, and we don't want to throw if it's not implemented.
       [$GetIterator$] throws errors in BOTH of the following cases: (a) no iterator protocol is
       implemented, (b) an iterator protocol is implemented, but isn't callable or its getter
       throws. [$GetMethod$] lets us ONLY throw in the latter case.

    1. If |asyncIteratorMethod|'s is undefined or null, then jump to the step labeled <a
       href=#from-iterable-conversion>From iterable</a>.

    1. Let |nextAlgorithm| be the following steps, given a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and an
       [=Iterator Record=] |iteratorRecord|:

         1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=] is
            [=AbortSignal/aborted=], then return.

         1. Let |nextPromise| be a {{Promise}}-or-undefined, initially undefined.

         1. Let |nextCompletion| be [$IteratorNext$](|iteratorRecord|).

            Note: We use [$IteratorNext$] here instead of [$IteratorStepValue$], because
            [$IteratorStepValue$] expects the iterator's `next()` method to return an object that
            can immediately be inspected for a value, whereas in the async iterator case, `next()`
            is expected to return a Promise/thenable (which we wrap in a Promise and react to to get
            that value).

         1. If |nextCompletion| is a [=throw completion=], then:

            1. [=Assert=]: |iteratorRecord|'s \[[Done]] is true.

            1. Set |nextPromise| to [=a promise rejected with=] |nextCompletion|'s \[[Value]].

         1. Otherwise, if |nextCompletion| is [=normal completion=], then set |nextPromise| to [=a
            promise resolved with=] |nextCompletion|'s \[[Value]].

            Note: This is done in case |nextCompletion|'s \[[Value]] is not *itself* already a
            {{Promise}}.

         1. [=promise/React=] to |nextPromise|:

             * If |nextPromise| was fulfilled with value |iteratorResult|, then:

               1. If [$Type$](|iteratorResult|) is not Object, then run |subscriber|'s
                  {{Subscriber/error()}} method with a {{TypeError}} and abort these steps.

               1. Let |done| be [$IteratorComplete$](|iteratorResult|).

               1. If |done| is a [=throw completion=], then run |subscriber|'s
                  {{Subscriber/error()}} method with |done|'s \[[Value]] and abort these steps.

               1. If |done|'s \[[Value]] is true, then run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}}
                  and abort these steps.

               1. Let |value| be [$IteratorValue$](|iteratorResult|).

               1. If |value| is a [=throw completion=], then run |subscriber|'s
                  {{Subscriber/error()}} method with |value|'s \[[Value]] and abort these steps.

               1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} given |value|'s \[[Value]].

               1. Run |nextAlgorithm| given |subscriber| and |iteratorRecord|.

             * If |nextPromise| was rejected with reason |r|, then run |subscriber|'s
               {{Subscriber/error()}} method given |r|.

    1. Return a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an algorithm that
       takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=] is
          [=AbortSignal/aborted=], then return.

       1. Let |iteratorRecordCompletion| be [$GetIterator$](|value|, async).

          Note: This both re-invokes any {{%Symbol.asyncIterator%}} method getters on |value|—note
          that whether this is desirable is an extreme corner case, but it matches test
          expectations; see <a href=https://github.com/WICG/observable/issues/127>issue#127</a> for
          discussion—and invokes the protocol itself to obtain an [=Iterator Record=].

       1. If |iteratorRecordCompletion| is a [=throw completion=], then run |subscriber|'s
          {{Subscriber/error()}} method with |iteratorRecordCompletion|'s \[[Value]] and abort these
          steps.

          Note: This means we invoke the {{Subscriber/error()}} method synchronously with respect to
          subscription, which is the only time this can happen for async iterables that are
          converted to {{Observable}}s. In all other cases, errors are propagated to the observer
          asynchronously, with microtask timing, by virtue of being wrapped in a rejected
          {{Promise}} that |nextAlgorithm| [=promise/reacts=] to. This synchronous-error-propagation
          behavior is consistent with language constructs, i.e., **for-await of** loops that invoke
          {{%Symbol.asyncIterator%}} and synchronously re-throw exceptions to catch blocks outside
          the loop, before any [$Await|Awaiting$] takes place.

       1. Let |iteratorRecord| be [=!=] |iteratorRecordCompletion|.

       1. [=Assert=]: |iteratorRecord| is an [=Iterator Record=].

       1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=] is
          [=AbortSignal/aborted=], then return.

       1. [=AbortSignal/add|Add the following abort algorithm=] to |subscriber|'s
          [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=]:

          1. Run [$AsyncIteratorClose$](|iteratorRecord|, [=NormalCompletion=](|subscriber|'s
             [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortSignal/abort reason=])).

       1. Run |nextAlgorithm| given |subscriber| and |iteratorRecord|.

    1. <i id=from-iterable-conversion><b>From iterable</b></i>: Let |iteratorMethod| be [=?=]
       [$GetMethod$](|value|, {{%Symbol.iterator%}}).

    1. If |iteratorMethod| is undefined, then jump to the step labeled <a
       href=#from-promise-conversion>From Promise</a>.

       Otherwise, return a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

         1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=] is
            [=AbortSignal/aborted=], then return.

         1. Let |iteratorRecordCompletion| be [$GetIterator$](|value|, sync).

         1. If |iteratorRecordCompletion| is a [=throw completion=], then run |subscriber|'s
            {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |iteratorRecordCompletion|'s \[[Value]], and abort
            these steps.

         1. Let |iteratorRecord| be [=!=] |iteratorRecordCompletion|.

         1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=] is
            [=AbortSignal/aborted=], then return.

         1. [=AbortSignal/add|Add the following abort algorithm=] to |subscriber|'s
            [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=]:

            1. Run [$IteratorClose$](|iteratorRecord|, [=NormalCompletion=](UNUSED)).

         1. [=iteration/While=] true:

            1. Let |next| be [$IteratorStepValue$](|iteratorRecord|).

            1. If |next| is a [=throw completion=], then run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}}
               method, given |next|'s \[[Value]], and [=iteration/break=].

            1. Set |next| to [=!=] to |next|.

            1. If |next| is done, then:

               1. [=Assert=]: |iteratorRecord|'s \[[Done]] is true.

               2. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}}.

               3. Return.

            1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} given |next|.

            1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=]
               is [=AbortSignal/aborted=], then [=iteration/break=].

    1. <i id=from-promise-conversion><b>From Promise</b></i>: If [$IsPromise$](|value|) is true,
       then:

       1. Return a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an algorithm
          that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

          1. [=promise/React=] to |value|:

             1. If |value| was fulfilled with value |v|, then:

                1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given |v|.

                1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

             1. If |value| was rejected with reason |r|, then run |subscriber|'s
                {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |r|.

    1. [=exception/Throw=] a {{TypeError}}.

<wpt>
  /dom/observable/tentative/observable-from.any.js
</wpt>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  To <dfn for=Observable>subscribe to an {{Observable}}</dfn> given an
  {{ObserverUnion}}-or-[=internal observer=] |observer|, and a {{SubscribeOptions}} |options|, run
  these steps:

  Note: We split this algorithm out from the Web IDL {{Observable/subscribe()}} method, so that spec
  prose can <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">subscribe</a> to an {{Observable}}
  without going through the Web IDL bindings. See <a
  href=https://github.com/w3c/IntersectionObserver/issues/464>w3c/IntersectionObserver#464</a> for
  similar context, where "internal" prose <span class=allow-2119>must</span> not go through Web IDL
  bindings on objects whose properties could be mutated by JavaScript. See
  [[#observable-returning-operators]] for usage of this.

    1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is a {{Window}} object, and its [=associated
       Document=] is not [=Document/fully active=], then return.

    1. Let |internal observer| be a new [=internal observer=].

    1. Process |observer| as follows:
       <ol id=process-observer>
         <li>
           <dl class="switch">
             <dt>If |observer| is an {{ObservableSubscriptionCallback}}</dt>
             <dd>Set |internal observer|'s [=internal observer/next steps=] to these steps that take
             an {{any}} |value|:

                 1. [=Invoke=] |observer| with «|value|» and "`report`".
             </dd>

             <dt>If |observer| is a {{SubscriptionObserver}}</dt>
             <dd>
               1. If |observer|'s {{SubscriptionObserver/next}} [=map/exists=], then set |internal
                  observer|'s [=internal observer/next steps=] to these steps that take an {{any}}
                  |value|:

                  1. [=Invoke=] |observer|'s {{SubscriptionObserver/next}} with «|value|» and
                     "`report`".

               1. If |observer|'s {{SubscriptionObserver/error}} [=map/exists=], then set |internal
                  observer|'s [=internal observer/error steps=] to these steps that take an {{any}}
                  |error|:

                  1. [=Invoke=] |observer|'s {{SubscriptionObserver/error}} with «|error|» and
                     "`report`".

               1. If |observer|'s {{SubscriptionObserver/complete}} [=map/exists=], then set
                  |internal observer|'s [=internal observer/complete steps=] to these steps:

                  1. [=Invoke=] |observer|'s {{SubscriptionObserver/complete}} with «» and
                     "`report`".
             </dd>

             <dt>If |observer| is an [=internal observer=]</dt>
             <dd>Set |internal observer| to |observer|.</dd>
           </dl>
         </li>
       </ol>

    1. [=Assert=]: The internal observer's steps do not let callback exceptions escape. Script
       callbacks report exceptions; native operator steps handle callback failures explicitly.

    1. If |options|'s {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} exists and is [=AbortSignal/aborted=], return.
    1. Set |internal observer|'s [=internal observer/consumer signal=] to that signal, if present.
    1. Append |internal observer| to [=this=]'s [=Observable/observations=].
    1. If [=this=] is a {{SharedObservable}} with a non-null [=SharedObservable/retained terminal=]:
       1. Install a [=internal observer/cancellation algorithm=] that marks |internal observer|
          canceled and inactive and removes it from [=this=]'s observations when its consumer signal aborts.
       1. For each |value| in a fixed copy of [=this=]'s [=SharedObservable/replay queue=]:
          1. If |internal observer| is inactive or canceled, break.
          1. Run its [=internal observer/next steps=] given |value|.
       1. Mark |internal observer| inactive and remove it from [=this=]'s observations.
       1. If it is not canceled, deliver the retained completion or error through its corresponding steps.
       1. Remove its cancellation algorithm from its consumer signal, if present, and return.
    1. Let |subscriber| be [=this=]'s [=SharedObservable/current subscriber=] if [=this=] is shared,
       or null otherwise.
    1. Let |start| be true if |subscriber| is null, and false otherwise.
    1. If |start| is true:
       1. Set |subscriber| to a new {{Subscriber}} whose [=Subscriber/owner=] is [=this=].
       1. If [=this=] is shared, set its [=SharedObservable/current subscriber=] to |subscriber|.
    1. Append |internal observer| to |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/internal observers=].
    1. Set |internal observer|'s [=internal observer/cancellation algorithm=] to these steps:
       1. [=Cancel an observation=] given |internal observer| and |subscriber|.
    1. If a consumer signal is present, [=AbortSignal/add|add=] that algorithm to it.
    1. If [=this=] is shared, iterate a fixed copy of its [=SharedObservable/replay queue=]:
       1. Before each value, if |subscriber| or |internal observer| is inactive, or |internal
          observer| is canceled, break.
       1. Run |internal observer|'s [=internal observer/next steps=] given that value.
    1. If |start| is false or |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, return.
    1. If [=this=]'s [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is a {{SubscribeCallback}}, [=invoke=] it
       with «|subscriber|» and "`rethrow`". If an exception is thrown, run |subscriber|'s
       {{Subscriber/error()}} method given that exception.
    1. Otherwise, run the native callback steps given |subscriber|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  To <dfn>cancel an observation</dfn> given an [=internal observer=] |observer| and a
  {{Subscriber}} |subscriber|:

    1. If |observer|'s [=internal observer/canceled=] is true, return.
    1. Set its [=internal observer/canceled=] to true and [=internal observer/active=] to false.
    1. Remove its cancellation algorithm from its consumer signal, if present.
    1. Let |owner| be |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/owner=].
    1. Remove |observer| from |owner|'s [=Observable/observations=] and |subscriber|'s
       [=Subscriber/internal observers=].
    1. If |subscriber| is inactive or its internal observers are not empty, return.
    1. If |owner| is shared:
       1. If its settings' {{ShareConfig/keepAliveOnLastUnsubscribe}} is true, return.
       1. Set its [=SharedObservable/current subscriber=] to null.
       1. If its settings' {{ShareConfig/closeOnLastUnsubscribe}} is true, set its
          [=SharedObservable/retained terminal=] to ("complete", undefined).
       1. Otherwise, clear its [=SharedObservable/replay queue=].
    1. [=Close a subscription=] given |subscriber| and the consumer signal's abort reason.

  Note: Last-unsubscribe settings apply only to cancellation of the final active observation of
  a running execution. They do not apply at construction or when completion/error detaches
  observers. Keep-alive does not prevent producer termination and teardown with zero observers.
</div>


<h3 id=operators>Operators</h3>

Every composition operator in this section returns an ordinary {{Observable}}, even on shared
input. Construction does not subscribe. Each subscription owns independent callback indices,
counters, queues, and inner subscriptions. The returned observable's declared sources are
[=collect shared sources=] given «the receiver», or «the receiver, the converted notifier» for
{{Observable/takeUntil()}}. Dynamic inner inputs of {{Observable/flatMap()}},
{{Observable/switchMap()}}, and {{Observable/catch()}} are not added to declared ancestry.

Once an operator knows an observation is no longer needed, it commits stopped state and detaches
that observation, running already registered cleanup, before downstream callbacks or replacement
work. Any callback or teardown can reenter. Internal observer steps are guarded by the observation's
active/canceled state; after invoking script, an operator checks its downstream Subscriber before
performing further work. No added scheduler or catch-up queue is used.

{{Observable/subscribe()}} is the only public consuming method. Promise-returning consumers
(`toArray`, `forEach`, `every`, `some`, `first`, `last`, `find`, and `reduce`) are not part of this
API. Promise inputs remain supported. `scan` and `takeLast` remain optional designs for later
consideration; they are not specified as methods here.

<h4 id=observable-from>{{Observable/from()}}</h4>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>from(|value|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Return the result of <a for=Observable lt="convert to an Observable">converting</a> |value|
       to an {{Observable}}. Rethrow any exceptions.
</div>

<h4 id=observable-returning-operators>{{Observable}}-returning operators</h4>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>takeUntil(|value|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |notifier| be the result of <a for=Observable lt="convert to an Observable">
       converting</a> |value| to an {{Observable}}.

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       <div class=note>
         Note that this method involves <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an
         Observable">Subscribing</a> to two {{Observable}}s: (1) |notifier|, and (2)
         |sourceObservable|. We "unsubscribe" from **both** of them in the following situations:

           1. |notifier| starts emitting values (either "next" or "error"). In this case, we
              unsubscribe from |notifier| since we got all we need from it, and no longer need it to
              keep producing values. We also unsubscribe from |sourceObservable|, because it no
              longer needs to produce values that get plumbed through this method's returned
              |observable|, because we're manually ending the subscription to |observable|, since
              |notifier| finally produced a value.

           1. |sourceObservable| either {{Subscriber/error()}}s or {{Subscriber/complete()}}s
              itself. In this case, we unsubscribe from |notifier| since we no longer need to listen
              for values it emits in order to determine when |observable| can stop mirroring values
              from |sourceObservable| (since |sourceObservable| ran to completion by itself).
              Unsubscribing from |sourceObservable| isn't necessary, since its subscription has been
              exhausted by itself.
       </div>

       1. Let |notifierObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

             Note: This will "unsubscribe" from |sourceObservable|, if it has been subscribed to by
             this point. This is because |sourceObservable| is subscribed to with the "outer"
             |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=] as
             an input signal, and that signal will get [=AbortSignal/signal abort|aborted=] when the
             "outer" |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} is called above (and below).

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

          Note: We do not specify [=internal observer/complete steps=], because if the |notifier|
          {{Observable}} completes itself, we do not need to complete the |subscriber| associated
          with the |observable| returned from this method. Rather, the |observable| will continue to
          mirror |sourceObservable| uninterrupted.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |notifier| given
          |notifierObserver| and |options|.

       1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/active=] is false, then return.

          Note: This means that |sourceObservable|'s [=Observable/subscribe callback=] will not even
          get invoked once, if |notifier| synchronously emits a value. If |notifier| only
          "completes" synchronously though (without emitting a "next" or "error" value), then
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/active=] will still be true, and we proceed to subscribe to
          |sourceObservable|, which |observable| will mirror uninterrupted.

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>value</var>.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>error</var>.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

          Note: |sourceObserver| is mostly a pass-through, mirroring everything that
          |sourceObservable| emits, with the exception of having the ability to unsubscribe from the
          |notifier| {{Observable}} in the case where |sourceObservable| is exhausted before
          |notifier| emits anything.

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.

  <wpt>
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-takeUntil.any.js
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-takeUntil.window.js
  </wpt>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>map(|mapper|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. Let |idx| be an {{unsigned long long}}, initially 0.

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: 1. Let |currentIndex| be |idx|, then increment |idx|.
             1. [=Invoke=] |mapper| with «the passed in <var ignore>value</var>, |currentIndex|» and
                "`rethrow`"; let |mappedValue| be the returned value.

                If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then
                run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.

             1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given |mappedValue|.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>error</var>.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.

  <wpt>
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-map.any.js
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-map.window.js
  </wpt>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>filter(|predicate|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. Let |idx| be an {{unsigned long long}}, initially 0.

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: 1. Let |currentIndex| be |idx|, then increment |idx|.
             1. [=Invoke=] |predicate| with «the passed in |value|, |currentIndex|» and "`rethrow`"; let
                |matches| be the returned value.

                If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then
                run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.

             1. If |matches| is true, then run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given
                |value|.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>error</var>.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.

  <wpt>
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-filter.any.js
  </wpt>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>take(|amount|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. Let |remaining| be |amount|.

       1. If |remaining| is 0, then run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method and abort
          these steps.

       1. Let |upstream| be a new {{AbortController}}.
       1. Add an abort algorithm to |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/signal}} that aborts |upstream|
          with the downstream abort reason. Remove this algorithm when |upstream| aborts.

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: 1. If |subscriber| is inactive or |remaining| is 0, return.
             1. Decrement |remaining|.
             1. If |remaining| is 0, abort |upstream| before proceeding.
             1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method with the passed in <var ignore>value</var>.
             1. If |remaining| is 0, run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>error</var>.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |upstream|'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.

  <wpt>
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-take.any.js
  </wpt>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>drop(|amount|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. Let |remaining| be |amount|.

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: 1. If |remaining| is &gt; 0, then decrement |remaining| and abort these steps.

             1. [=Assert=]: |remaining| is 0.

             1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method with the passed in <var
                ignore>value</var>.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>error</var>.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.

  <wpt>
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-drop.any.js
  </wpt>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>flatMap(|mapper|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. Let |idx| be an {{unsigned long long}}, initially 0.

       1. Let |outerSubscriptionHasCompleted| to a [=boolean=], initially false.

       1. Let |queue| be a new [=list=] of {{any}} values, initially empty.

          Note: This |queue| is used to store any {{Observable}}s emitted by |sourceObservable|,
          while |observable| is currently subscribed to an {{Observable}} emitted earlier by
          |sourceObservable| that has not yet been exhausted.

       1. Let |activeInnerSubscription| be a [=boolean=], initially false.

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: 1. If |activeInnerSubscription| is true, then:

                1. [=list/Append=] |value| to |queue|.

                   Note: This |value| will eventually be processed once the {{Observable}} that is
                   currently subscribed-to (as indicated by |activeInnerSubscription|) is exhausted.

             1. Otherwise:

                1. Set |activeInnerSubscription| to true.

                1. Run the [=flatmap process next value steps=] with |value|, |subscriber|,
                   |mapper|, and <b>references</b> to all of the following: |queue|,
                   |activeInnerSubscription|, |outerSubscriptionHasCompleted|, and |idx|.

                   <div class=note>
                     <p>Note: This [=flatmap process next value steps=] will subscribe to the
                     {{Observable}} derived from |value| (if one such can be derived) and keep
                     processing values from it until its subscription becomes inactive (either by
                     error or completion). If this "inner" {{Observable}} completes, then the
                     processing steps will recursively invoke themselves with the next {{any}} in
                     |queue|.</p>

                     <p>If no such value [=list/exists=], then the processing steps will terminate,
                     <b>unsetting</b> |activeInnerSubscription|, so that future values emitted from
                     |sourceObservable| are processed correctly.</p>
                   </div>

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>error</var>.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: 1. Set |outerSubscriptionHasCompleted| to true.

                Note: If |activeInnerSubscription| is true, then the below step will *not* complete
                |subscriber|. In that case, the [=flatmap process next value steps=] will be
                responsible for completing |subscriber| when |queue| is [=list/empty=], after the
                "inner" subscription becomes inactive.

             1. If |activeInnerSubscription| is false and |queue| is [=list/empty=], run
                |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn>flatmap process next value steps</dfn>, given an {{any}} |value|, a {{Subscriber}}
  |subscriber|, a {{Mapper}} |mapper|, and <b>references</b> to all of the following: a [=list=] of
  {{any}} values |queue|, a [=boolean=] |activeInnerSubscription|, a [=boolean=]
  |outerSubscriptionHasCompleted|, and an {{unsigned long long}} |idx|:

    1. If |subscriber| is inactive, return.
    1. Let |currentIndex| be |idx|, then increment |idx|.
    1. Let |mappedResult| be the result of [=invoking=] |mapper| with «|value|, |currentIndex|» and
       "`rethrow`".

       If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then run
       |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.

    1. If |subscriber| is inactive, return.
    1. Let |innerObservable| be the result of <a for=Observable lt="convert to an Observable">converting</a> |mappedResult|.

       If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then run
       |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.


     1. Let |innerObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

        : [=internal observer/next steps=]
        :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given the passed in |value|.

        : [=internal observer/error steps=]
        :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
           ignore>error</var>.

        : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
        :: 1. If |queue| is not empty, then:

              1. Let |nextValue| be the first item in |queue|; [=list/remove=] remove this item from
                 |queue|.

              1. Run [=flatmap process next value steps=] given |nextValue|, |subscriber|, |mapper|,
                 and <b>references</b> to |queue|, |activeInnerSubscription|,
                 |outerSubscriptionHasCompleted|, and |idx|.

           1. Otherwise:

              1. Set |activeInnerSubscription| to false.

                 Note: Because |activeInnerSubscription| is a reference, this has the effect of
                 ensuring that all subsequent values emitted from the "outer" {{Observable}} (called
                 <var ignore>sourceObservable</var>.

              1. If |outerSubscriptionHasCompleted| is true, run |subscriber|'s
                 {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

                 Note: This means the "outer" {{Observable}} has already completed, but did not
                 proceed to complete |subscriber| yet because there was at least one more pending
                 "inner" {{Observable}} (i.e., |innerObservable|) that had already been queued and
                 had not yet completed. Until right now!

     1. Let |innerOptions| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
        |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

     1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |innerObservable| given
        |innerObserver| and |innerOptions|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>switchMap(|mapper|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |source| be [=this=].
    1. Return a new ordinary {{Observable}}, with declared sources collected from «|source|»,
       whose subscription steps given |subscriber| are:
       1. Let |index| be 0, |outerDone| be false, and |current| be null.
       1. Subscribe to |source| with |subscriber|'s signal and these internal observer steps:
          : next steps, given |value|
          :: 1. Let |previous| be |current|.
             1. Let |replacement| be a new {{AbortController}}, canceled when |subscriber|'s
                signal aborts; remove that abort algorithm when |replacement| aborts.
             1. Set |current| to |replacement| before aborting |previous|, if it is not null.
             1. If |subscriber| is inactive or |current| is not |replacement|, return.
             1. Let |currentIndex| be |index|, then increment |index|.
             1. Invoke |mapper| with «|value|, |currentIndex|». On exception, error |subscriber|
                with that exception and return.
             1. If |subscriber| is inactive or |current| is not |replacement|, return.
             1. Convert the mapper's result to an Observable. On exception, error |subscriber|
                with that exception and return.
             1. If |subscriber| is inactive or |current| is not |replacement|, return.
             1. Subscribe to the converted observable with |replacement|'s signal, forwarding
                next and error to |subscriber|, and these completion steps:
                1. If |current| is not |replacement|, return.
                1. Set |current| to null, then abort |replacement|.
                1. If |outerDone| is true, complete |subscriber|.
          : error steps, given |reason|
          :: Error |subscriber| with |reason|.
          : complete steps
          :: Set |outerDone| to true. If |current| is null, complete |subscriber|.

  Note: Publishing the replacement before aborting the old observation prevents reentrant cleanup
  from being overwritten by an older switch. Cleanup finishes before invoking the replacement
  mapper. If cleanup or mapping causes a newer switch, the superseded switch does not subscribe.
  The current identity also prevents an old completion from clearing a newer inner observation.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>inspect(|inspectorUnion|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |subscribe callback| be a {{VoidFunction}}-or-null, initially null.

    1. Let |next callback| be a {{ObservableSubscriptionCallback}}-or-null, initially null.

    1. Let |error callback| be a {{ObservableSubscriptionCallback}}-or-null, initially null.

    1. Let |complete callback| be a {{VoidFunction}}-or-null, initially null.

    1. Let |abort callback| be a {{ObservableInspectorAbortHandler}}-or-null, initially null.

    1. Process |inspectorUnion| as follows:
         <dl class="switch">
           <dt>If |inspectorUnion| is an {{ObservableSubscriptionCallback}}</dt>
           <dd>
             1. Set |next callback| to |inspectorUnion|.

           <dt>If |inspectorUnion| is an {{ObservableInspector}}</dt>
           <dd>
             1. If {{ObservableInspector/subscribe}} [=map/exists=] in |inspectorUnion|, then set
                |subscribe callback| to it.

             1. If {{ObservableInspector/next}} [=map/exists=] in |inspectorUnion|, then set |next
                callback| to it.

             1. If {{ObservableInspector/error}} [=map/exists=] in |inspectorUnion|, then set |error
                callback| to it.

             1. If {{ObservableInspector/complete}} [=map/exists=] in |inspectorUnion|, then set
                |complete callback| to it.

             1. If {{ObservableInspector/abort}} [=map/exists=] in |inspectorUnion|, then set |abort
                callback| to it.
           </dd>
         </dl>

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. If |subscribe callback| is not null, then [=invoke=] it with «» and "`rethrow`".

          If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then run
          |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.

          Note: The result of this is that |sourceObservable| is never subscribed to.

       1. If |abort callback| is not null, then [=AbortSignal/add|add the following abort
          algorithm=] to |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s
          [=AbortController/signal=]:

          1. [=Invoke=] |abort callback| with «|subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription
             controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=]'s [=AbortSignal/abort reason=]» and "`report`".

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: 1. If |next callback| is not null, then [=invoke=] |next callback| with «the passed in
                |value|» and "`rethrow`".

                If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>,
                then:

                  1. [=AbortSignal/Remove=] |abort callback| from |subscriber|'s
                     [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

                     Note: This step is important, because the |abort callback| is only meant to be
                     called for *consumer-initiated* unsubscriptions. When the producer terminates
                     the subscription (via |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} or
                     {{Subscriber/complete()}} methods) like below, we have to ensure that |abort
                     callback| is not run.

                     Issue: This matches Chromium's implementation, but consider holding a reference
                     to the originally-passed-in {{SubscribeOptions}}'s {{SubscribeOptions/signal}}
                     and just invoking |abort callback| when *it* aborts. The result is likely the
                     same, but needs investigation.

                  1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these
                     steps.

             1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method with the passed in |value|.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: 1. [=AbortSignal/Remove=] |abort callback| from |subscriber|'s
                [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

             1. If |error callback| is not null, then [=invoke=] |error callback| with «the passed
                in |error|» and "`rethrow`".

                If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then
                run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.

             1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in |error|.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: 1. [=AbortSignal/Remove=] |abort callback| from |subscriber|'s
                [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

             1. If |complete callback| is not null, then [=invoke=] |complete callback| with «»
                and "`rethrow`".

                If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then
                run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.

             1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.

  <wpt>
    /dom/observable/tentative/observable-inspect.any.js
  </wpt>
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>catch(|callback|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>value</var>.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          ::
             1. [=Invoke=] |callback| with «the passed in <var ignore>error</var>» and "`rethrow`".
                Let |result| be the returned value.

                If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then
                run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} with |E|, and abort these steps.

             1. If |subscriber| is inactive, return.
             1. Let |innerObservable| be the result of <a for=Observable lt="convert to an Observable">converting</a> |result|.

                If <a spec=webidl lt="an exception was thrown">an exception |E| was thrown</a>, then
                run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given |E|, and abort these steps.


             1. Let |innerObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

                : [=internal observer/next steps=]
                :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given the passed in <var
                   ignore>value</var>.

                : [=internal observer/error steps=]
                :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
                   ignore>error</var>.

                : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
                :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

             1. Let |innerOptions| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}}
                is |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s
                [=AbortController/signal=].

             1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |innerObservable|
                given |innerObserver| and |innerOptions|.

                Note: We're free to subscribe to |innerObservable| here without first
                "unsubscribing" from |sourceObservable|, and without fear that |sourceObservable|
                will keep emitting values, because all of this is happening inside of the [=internal
                observer/error steps=] associated with |sourceObservable|. This means
                |sourceObservable| has already completed its subscription and will no longer produce
                any values, and we are free to safely switch our source of values to
                |innerObservable|.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=Observable method><code>finally(|callback|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. Let |sourceObservable| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}} whose [=Observable/subscribe callback=] is an
       algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and does the following:

       1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/addTeardown()}} method with |callback|.

       1. Let |sourceObserver| be a new [=internal observer=], initialized as follows:

          : [=internal observer/next steps=]
          :: Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method, given the passed in <var
             ignore>value</var>.

          : [=internal observer/error steps=]
          :: 1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/error()}} method, given the passed in <var
                ignore>error</var>.

          : [=internal observer/complete steps=]
          :: 1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/complete()}} method.

       1. Let |options| be a new {{SubscribeOptions}} whose {{SubscribeOptions/signal}} is
          |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=].

       1. <a for=Observable lt="subscribe to an Observable">Subscribe</a> to |sourceObservable|
          given |sourceObserver| and |options|.

    1. Return |observable|.
</div>


<h2 id=event-target-integration>{{EventTarget}} integration</h2>

<pre class=idl>
dictionary ObservableEventListenerOptions {
  boolean capture = false;
  boolean passive;
};

partial interface EventTarget {
  Observable when(DOMString type, optional ObservableEventListenerOptions options = {});
};
</pre>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn for=EventTarget method><code>when(|type|, |options|)</code></dfn> method steps are:

    1. If [=this=]'s [=relevant global object=] is a {{Window}} object, and its [=associated
       Document=] is not [=Document/fully active=], then return.

    1. Let |event target| be [=this=].

    1. Let |observable| be a [=new=] {{Observable}}, initialized as follows:

       : [=Observable/subscribe callback=]
       :: An algorithm that takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and runs these steps:

            1. If |event target| is null, abort these steps.

               Note: This is meant to capture the fact that |event target| can be garbage collected
               by the time this algorithm runs upon subscription.

            1. If |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=]
               is [=AbortSignal/aborted=], abort these steps.

            1. [=Add an event listener=] with |event target| and an [=event listener=] defined as
               follows:

               : [=event listener/type=]
               :: |type|

               : [=event listener/callback=]
               :: The result of creating a new Web IDL {{EventListener}} instance representing a
                  reference to a function of one argument of type {{Event}} |event|. This function
                  executes the [=observable event listener invoke algorithm=] given |subscriber| and
                  |event|.

               : [=event listener/capture=]
               :: |options|'s {{ObservableEventListenerOptions/capture}}

               : [=event listener/passive=]
               :: |options|'s {{ObservableEventListenerOptions/passive}} if this member
                  [=map/exists=]; null otherwise.

               : [=event listener/once=]
               :: false

               : [=event listener/signal=]
               :: |subscriber|'s [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=]

                  Note: This ensures that the [=event listener=] is cleaned up when
                  [=Subscriber/subscription controller=]'s [=AbortController/signal=] is
                  [=AbortSignal/aborted=], regardless of an engine's ownership model.

    1. Return |observable|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
  The <dfn>observable event listener invoke algorithm</dfn> takes a {{Subscriber}} |subscriber| and
  an {{Event}} |event|, and runs these steps:

    1. Run |subscriber|'s {{Subscriber/next()}} method with |event|.
</div>

<wpt>
  /dom/observable/tentative/observable-event-target.any.js
  /dom/observable/tentative/observable-event-target.window.js
</wpt>


<h2 id=security-and-privacy>Security & Privacy Considerations</h2>

Event observation exposes the same events as ordinary DOM listeners and does not grant new
cross-origin access. Sharing can retain application data and resources longer than one consumer's
observation. Replay is bounded by value count, not object size; permanent closure retains replay
and terminal reasons. Keep-alive can retain a running resource with zero observations. Producers
choose these policies before exposing a shared instance.

Declared source references intentionally permit upstream observation and replay inspection.
Subscriber counts expose current participation in an object already made available to script;
they are snapshots and not synchronization primitives. No automatic discovery of hidden resources
or dynamic ancestry is performed. Frozen arrays do not freeze their referenced values.

Replaying events repeats consumer callbacks, which may repeat application side effects. It cannot
retroactively cancel a DOM default action. Applications can share parsed data instead of
single-consumption resources such as response bodies. An observable instance does not provide
cross-instance, cross-tab, or server-side request deduplication.

See also:

 * [TAG Security/Privacy
   Questionnaire](https://github.com/WICG/observable/blob/master/security-privacy-questionnaire.md)

